{
  "statement": "Frontier AI should be gated by compute-threshold licensing as a precautionary floor — not because catastrophe is certain, but because some frontier capabilities cannot be recalled once released. The question before this committee is not speed versus caution. It is reversible mistake versus unrecoverable one. We license pharmaceuticals before they reach patients and certify aircraft before they carry passengers for a single reason: when harm cannot be undone, you do not wait for the body count to write the rule. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA let Boeing certify its own flight-control software. The lesson is not that certification is burdensome. The lesson is that self-certification before an irreversible failure is how the failure happens.\n\nCompute is the one chokepoint measurable enough to make accountability enforceable rather than nominal. Both the Biden Executive Order 14110 and the EU AI Act anchored their reporting triggers to floating-point thresholds — roughly 10^25 to 10^26 operations. Hardware export controls under the EAR already prove that advanced chips are trackable at the fabrication and cloud layer, where the United States and its allies dominate. I will concede the strongest objection before my opponents raise it: compute is a proxy, not a risk measure. Distillation and algorithmic efficiency erode any fixed number over time. So the threshold cannot be a frozen statute. It must be a recalibrating floor, indexed to capability and revised on a schedule, the way the FDA revises its standards as the science moves.\n\nA license signed once is a checkbox, and a checkbox is compliance theater. The discipline comes from liability. When the Price-Anderson Act forced nuclear operators to carry mandatory insurance and pool catastrophic exposure, it did not slow the industry — it priced safety into every operating decision for sixty years. Frontier developers who cross the threshold must internalize the financial cost of the harms they create. Strict liability paired with required insurance generates continuous market pressure that no one-time permit can. I will name the hard part honestly: attributing a specific harm to a specific model is legally unsettled, and that attribution problem is the design challenge this debate must solve — not a reason to abandon accountability.\n\nNow the objection my own position must answer: does gating build a moat for incumbents? Here is the distinction that matters. Capability concentration already exists. A handful of well-capitalized labs — OpenAI, Anthropic, Google DeepMind — already command the frontier, opaquely, with no external auditor in the room. Gating does not create that concentration. The moat is already dug. What gating plus mandatory transparency does is put an inspector inside it. I will own the cost squarely: concentrating the off-switch invites incumbent lock-in and even authoritarian capture. That is precisely why the regime must pair licensing with transparency mandates, not protected secrecy. A gate without a window is a worse outcome than no gate at all.\n\nThe competitiveness argument cuts the other way. The real strategic risk is not a careful America falling behind. It is a global race to the bottom on safety standards, where the first unrecoverable failure is shared by everyone downstream. Compute chokepoints the United States dominates at the hardware layer are leverage for international coordination — the same way export controls became leverage. So I will put the burden where it belongs. To anyone who would cross the irreversibility-relevant threshold without a gate, answer one question: what is your rollback plan, and who has the authority to trigger it? If there is no answer, there is no business deploying the capability. The silence is the argument.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "Compute licensing is a gate before the irreversible threshold — you certify the aircraft before it flies, not after it crashes."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Like Price-Anderson for nuclear, strict liability plus insurance prices safety into every decision rather than relying on a one-time permit."},
    {"symbol": "🔍", "tooltip": "The frontier moat already exists; gating plus transparency puts an inspector inside it rather than building a new wall."}
  ],
