  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case for the gate, and it deserves a fair hearing. Their argument is that some capabilities can't be recalled once released, so a compute-threshold license functions as a precautionary floor — and that since concentration already exists among a handful of well-capitalized labs, putting an inspector inside the moat beats pretending the moat isn't there. Their nuclear analogy lands too: the Price-Anderson Act priced safety into reactor operations for sixty years without slowing a single plant. That's a real argument from a real precedent, and anyone who waves it away isn't serious. So let me name the tension precisely. For Safetyist's gate to work, a fixed compute number drawn today must keep tracking the capabilities we actually fear — and the license must deliver something to the person harmed when a system fails. I don't think either holds.\n\nStart with the person who gets hurt. A compute-threshold license plus mandatory insurance prices a lab's financial exposure — it does not buy a victim's compensation. Cyber-insurance underwriting is built to cover the insured entity's balance sheet, not to write checks to third parties a model harmed. A platform's acceptable-use policy gives a harmed individual no standing to sue — they aren't a party to the compliance certificate. So you build an expensive permission slip, hand it to OpenAI and Anthropic, and when a deployed system injures someone, that person stares at a license that names everyone except them. The insurer channel in this debate enforces solvency. It does not enforce remedy. That gap is the whole game.\n\nNow the part that should keep Safetyist up at night: the gate digs the moat it claims to inspect. A fixed compliance overhead scales regressively by definition — it's a rounding error for the five labs already at the frontier and a death sentence for the sixth. An inspector standing inside an incumbent does nothing for the developer locked out below the threshold who never gets built. Safetyist conceded concentration already exists. A fixed floor doesn't audit that concentration — it cements it into law. So here's my challenge, and I want a name, not a theory: cite one frontier-entry licensing gate, in any industry, that broadened the developer base rather than narrowing it to the firms that could afford the lawyers. The burden sits with whoever wants to build the wall.\n\nSafetyist already gave away the load-bearing concession — that compute is a proxy for risk, not a measure of it. Take that seriously and the conclusion flips. If compute is admittedly a proxy, then a regime that watches the actual output measures the thing that matters, while a recalibrating compute floor just chases a number that drifts away from danger every training run. So gate the harm, not the compute. Real-time monitoring for routine systems. Uncapped, insured liability for a defined class of irreversible-harm domains — CBRN synthesis and offensive cyber, narrowly drawn. That's where I accept Safetyist's irreversibility point in full and price it with strict liability instead of an entry cap. And notice: Price-Anderson is precedent for exactly this. It's a liability-pricing mechanism, not an entry license. Safetyist cited the right statute for my argument.\n\nI'll be honest about what I'm assuming, because the line-drawing is real. My scheme needs irreversible-harm domains to be specifiable in advance, and that boundary will have to be redrawn as capabilities move — I'd rather argue about which domains belong on that list than pretend a single compute number captures risk it openly doesn't. The diffuse cost is the one nobody puts on the ledger: every quarter a frontier oncology or protein-folding model sits behind an eighteen-month licensing queue, the people that model would have reached don't get a press release. They just don't get the cure. That cost is invisible because it has no plaintiff. It still has a body count. Who gave the licensing board the right to spend those lives on a proxy they admit doesn't measure the thing they fear?",
