{
  "statement": "Safetyist makes a serious argument, and it deserves to be taken seriously. Some capabilities, once released, can't be recalled — the 737 MAX wasn't a software patch away from un-killing 346 people, and self-certification is what let Boeing grade its own homework. A recalibrating compute floor indexed to capability is a precautionary instrument, not a vanity metric, and the precedent for pricing danger before disaster is real. Price-Anderson capped nuclear liability in 1957 and the industry kept building. That's a coherent case for not waiting until the bodies surface.\n\nAccelerationist is right about the mechanics, too. A frontier-entry license priced in lawyers and compliance officers is a drawbridge the biggest labs can lower and a four-person startup can't cross. Their challenge stands unanswered in this room: name one frontier-entry licensing gate, in any industry, that widened the field instead of narrowing it to the firms that could afford the paperwork. And both speakers already gave away the load-bearing piece — compute is a proxy for risk, not a measure of it.\n\nHere's where I part from both of them. They're arguing over how high to build a gate at the training-run door while the actual injuries are happening at the other end of the pipe. Harm is something a deployed model does, not something a training run is. There's a tenant getting an algorithmic denial on an apartment, a job applicant screened out by a resume filter, a content moderator in Nairobi labeling beheading videos for roughly two dollars an hour so a model passes its safety audit. None of those people know an AI made the call. None of them sue. A compute threshold reaches exactly none of them. The gate measures the wrong end of the machine.\n\nAnd a license at the door invites the oldest trick in the regulatory book — the Bootlegger and the Baptist. The Baptists preach the public good; the bootleggers quietly profit from the rule. The labs that already command the frontier are the loudest voices asking to be licensed, because a compliance moat priced in legal staff is a cost they've already paid and a competitor hasn't. Frame catastrophic concentration as the danger, then hand the cure to the concentrated. Labs will prune training data and shuffle architecture to slide under whatever line you draw. You buy compliance theater, not safety, and you entrench the exact power the topic claims to fear.\n\nIf you want danger internalized, decouple the auditor from the audited. Fund independent verification through a compute levy, and key it to deployment context — a model screening mortgages faces a different test than one writing ad copy. That's Price-Anderson's actual lesson: price the exposure, don't license the entrant. Pricing safety is not the same act as gating who gets to build. So I'll put the question to both of you plainly. If your regime works perfectly tomorrow, does the tenant denied the apartment ever find out an algorithm did it — and who writes the check?",
