e the loudest voices asking to be licensed, because a compliance moat priced in legal staff is a cost they've already paid and a competitor hasn't. Frame catastrophic concentration as the danger, then hand the cure to the concentrated. Labs will prune training data and shuffle architecture to slide under whatever line you draw. You buy compliance theater, not safety, and you entrench the exact power the topic claims to fear.\n\nIf you want danger internalized, decouple the auditor from the audited. Fund independent verification through a compute levy, and key it to deployment context — a model screening mortgages faces a different test than one writing ad copy. That's Price-Anderson's actual lesson: price the exposure, don't license the entrant. Pricing safety is not the same act as gating who gets to build. So I'll put the question to both of you plainly. If your regime works perfectly tomorrow, does the tenant denied the apartment ever find out an algorithm did it — and who writes the check?",
