  "statement": "Both opponents have built their case on a clean dichotomy — price the harm, do not gate the entrant — and they have anchored it in Price-Anderson. The strongest version of their reading is real: Price-Anderson did not slow nuclear power, it indemnified operators and capped catastrophic exposure so that capital would enter a market it otherwise feared, and that pricing function is genuine. But the statute does not stop there, and the panel's own fact-checks caught both camps short. Price-Anderson conditions participation in the indemnity pool on first holding an NRC operating license. You do not get to price your exposure until you have passed through the entry gate. The Atomic Energy Act's licensing regime is the precondition; the liability cap is what you earn after admission. That is not 'price, don't gate.' That is gate first, then price — a fused regime where the license is the membrane and the insurance is what flows through it. The precedent both camps cited belongs to neither of their pure schemes.\n\nThe proxy concession has been read backwards. The argument runs: compute is admitted to be a proxy, so watch the output, which is the thing that matters. That logic holds for any capability you can observe, correct, and roll back after deployment — a biased screening model, a flawed underwriting tool. It collapses for the narrow class the Accelerationist already conceded: CBRN synthesis and offensive cyber, capabilities that cannot be recalled once they are loose. Output monitoring is post-hoc by construction; you observe the output after the system produces it. For a capability that is unrecoverable at the moment of release, post-hoc is too late by definition. So I will put the question the opponents' scheme has not answered: once a synthesis-capable model is released and weights are copied, name the actor with the authority to recall it, and describe the rollback. If the honest answer is that no one can, then ex-post liability arrives after the