ompute is the wrong end when it is my anchor and the right end when it funds theirs. That is not a contradiction I am pointing out to score a point; it is evidence that compute is unavoidable as an enforcement anchor even for those who attack it, because it is the one input that is physically scarce, traceable through export-control channels, and measurable before a model exists.\n\nThe incumbent-moat objection deserves a straight answer rather than a dodge. A flat compliance fee priced in legal staff is regressive — it is a cost the largest labs have paid and a startup has not, and a gate built that way digs the moat its critics fear. So do not build it that way. Scale the obligation to capability, not to a flat entry toll: a floor that binds only at frontier-scale training runs leaves the small developer below the threshold entirely untouched, while obligations rise with the capability that actually carries irreversible risk. I will concede the ceiling the Accelerationist demanded: I cannot name a frontier-entry licensing regime that demonstrably broadened its developer base, and that history should make anyone cautious. But the question that decides this debate is narrower than the moat. It is whether any ex-ante instrument is justified for the irreversible-capability class the Accelerationist has already granted deserves special treatment. If ex-post liability and output monitoring both arrive after the unrecoverable event, then for that class the gate is not one option among several. It is the only tool that acts in time.",
