use narrow, high-stakes capability comes from architecture and data, not raw scale. Distillation moves frontier capability into small models continuously — the EU AI Act's own systemic threshold sits at 10^25 FLOPS, a line that distillation and algorithmic efficiency walk under a little more each quarter. The dangerous synthesis model of 2027 is not the trillion-parameter giant in the licensed data center. It is the distilled, specialized model someone runs on a cluster the gate never sees. So the gate guards an emptying chokepoint while criminalizing the legitimate frontier — the worst of both: it misses the weapon and it taxes the cure.\n\nIf you want to reach the actor who builds the irreversible thing, you reach the physical anchor and the adversary directly — export controls on the chips, interdiction at the border, secured-weights mandates that make exfiltration a federal crime. That's the EAR dual-use playbook, and it binds the traceable input and the actual threat actor rather than a precondition-to-build license on domestic developers who file honestly. Compute is a legitimate anchor for those physical levers. It is not a legitimate anchor for an entry permit, because the entry permit only constrains the law-abiding. And the capability-scaled version doesn't fix this — a floor that rises with the frontier is a moat that tracks the frontier, entrenching the same five firms already past it. The opposing side has already conceded it cannot name one frontier-entry license in any industry that widened the developer base. A moving threshold doesn't widen it; it just moves the wall.\n\nSo here is the question back: name the actor the gate stops that export control, interdiction, and secured-weights mandates do not — given that the person building the weapon was never going to file for the license, and the capability is sliding below your threshold while we argue about how high to set it. If you can't, the gate isn't catching the irreversible harm. It's just standing in front of the people building the medicine.",
