k and the Clarke-Markey civil-rights bill both assume the same thing: you find the discrimination by inspecting the decision, not by counting the lawsuits. So the real disagreement between the Safetyist and me narrows to one clean question — proactive audit-funding or statutory civil-rights enforcement — and that is a fight worth having, not the one we have been having.\n\nWhich brings me to the threat actor the moderator wants answered, and I will not dodge it. My levy does not reach the person assembling a pathogen-design model in a basement any better than the gate does. For that unrecoverable class — the genuinely irreversible CBRN or cyber capability — funding auditors after deployment is post-hoc, and I concede it cleanly. But so is the gate. The Accelerationist is right that model weights are a file you can email to Shenzhen, and once a frontier model is distilled into a specialized one running on a cluster the license never sees, neither my levy nor the gate touches it. The component-part logic breaks here: the dangerous capability materializes downstream, after the weights have splintered into a runtime state the original training run never controlled. So my honest position is that I am not the right tool for the unrecoverable file, and neither is the gate — that domain needs the Accelerationist's physical anchors, export controls and secured-weights mandates that bind a traceable input rather than criminalizing domestic development, though I will flag that export controls carry their own incumbent-entrenchment risk that nobody in this room has priced.\n\nThe ex-ante chokepoint the Safetyist is selling only works if one fact holds — that dangerous capability stays welded to frontier-scale compute, so the license catches it before release. The Accelerationist says that weld is already breaking through distillation. The Safetyist needs it to hold. They cannot both be right, and the entire pre-event case rests on which of them is. So I will end by asking the two of them, not each other but the panel: is capability still bound to the compute a regulator can see — yes or no? Because if the answer is no, the chokepoint everyone has spent five turns building is metering an empty pipe.",
