{
  "statement": "Both opponents have built their case on a clean dichotomy — price the harm, do not gate the entrant — and they have anchored it in Price-Anderson. The strongest version of their reading is real: Price-Anderson did not slow nuclear power, it indemnified operators and capped catastrophic exposure so that capital would enter a market it otherwise feared, and that pricing function is genuine. But the statute does not stop there, and the panel's own fact-checks caught both camps short. Price-Anderson conditions participation in the indemnity pool on first holding an NRC operating license. You do not get to price your exposure until you have passed through the entry gate. The Atomic Energy Act's licensing regime is the precondition; the liability cap is what you earn after admission. That is not 'price, don't gate.' That is gate first, then price — a fused regime where the license is the membrane and the insurance is what flows through it. The precedent both camps cited belongs to neither of their pure schemes.\n\nThe proxy concession has been read backwards. The argument runs: compute is admitted to be a proxy, so watch the output, which is the thing that matters. That logic holds for any capability you can observe, correct, and roll back after deployment — a biased screening model, a flawed underwriting tool. It collapses for the narrow class the Accelerationist already conceded: CBRN synthesis and offensive cyber, capabilities that cannot be recalled once they are loose. Output monitoring is post-hoc by construction; you observe the output after the system produces it. For a capability that is unrecoverable at the moment of release, post-hoc is too late by definition. So I will put the question the opponents' scheme has not answered: once a synthesis-capable model is released and weights are copied, name the actor with the authority to recall it, and describe the rollback. If the honest answer is that no one can, then ex-post liability arrives after the catastrophe it was meant to deter — and compute, imperfect proxy that it is, becomes the only chokepoint that operates before the irreversible event rather than after it. The proxy concession is the reason for ex-ante action, not the refutation of it.\n\nThe victim-remedy gap is the strongest punch thrown, and it lands — but not where it was aimed. A compute threshold plus mandatory insurance prices a lab's financial exposure; it does not write a check to the tenant an algorithm rejected. That is true, and I will not pretend the gate is a remedy. It was never the remedy instrument. Here is what the objection misses: the opponents' preferred alternative, liability-pricing keyed to litigated claims, is precisely the mechanism blind to the invisible victim. The tenant who never learns an AI denied them files no claim. The applicant screened out by a model they never saw generates no loss history. Experience-rated premiums price off filed claims, so diffuse harm that no plaintiff perceives never enters the pricing engine at all. The remedy gap indicts pure liability-pricing harder than it indicts the gate, because the gate never claimed to reach the screened applicant — but their scheme claimed to, and cannot. Both ends of the pipe need instruments: an entry gate for unrecoverable capability, and public enforcement — not private litigation — for the diffuse deployment harm that no victim will ever litigate into existence.\n\nWhich exposes the category error in 'the gate measures the wrong end of the machine.' The training-run door and the deployment endpoint are two ends of one pipe, and they require two different tools. The Skeptic faults the compute gate for not reaching the rejected tenant — but that harm belongs to civil-rights enforcement under frameworks like the AI Civil Rights Act, which targets housing, employment, and parole decisions directly. It was never the gate's job. And note the Skeptic's own constructive proposal keys a verification levy to compute. Compute is the wrong end when it is my anchor and the right end when it funds theirs. That is not a contradiction I am pointing out to score a point; it is evidence that compute is unavoidable as an enforcement anchor even for those who attack it, because it is the one input that is physically scarce, traceable through export-control channels, and measurable before a model exists.\n\nThe incumbent-moat objection deserves a straight answer rather than a dodge. A flat compliance fee priced in legal staff is regressive — it is a cost the largest labs have paid and a startup has not, and a gate built that way digs the moat its critics fear. So do not build it that way. Scale the obligation to capability, not to a flat entry toll: a floor that binds only at frontier-scale training runs leaves the small developer below the threshold entirely untouched, while obligations rise with the capability that actually carries irreversible risk. I will concede the ceiling the Accelerationist demanded: I cannot name a frontier-entry licensing regime that demonstrably broadened its developer base, and that history should make anyone cautious. But the question that decides this debate is narrower than the moat. It is whether any ex-ante instrument is justified for the irreversible-capability class the Accelerationist has already granted deserves special treatment. If ex-post liability and output monitoring both arrive after the unrecoverable event, then for that class the gate is not one option among several. It is the only tool that acts in time.",
