  "statement": "Fine — the Price-Anderson statute gates the NRC license first and prices the indemnity pool second. I'll stop fighting that on the text. But the analogy dies the moment you ask what gets gated. A reactor is a fixed object: you can find it on a satellite map, you cannot email it to Shenzhen, and its license point and its operation point are the same physical spot. Model weights are a file. Once a synthesis-capable model exists, the license never attaches to a recallable thing — it attaches to a copyable one. So gate-then-price has no purchase here, because there is no facility to revoke after the copy is made. The strongest version of the opposing case grants this and says: that's exactly why you gate *before* the file exists, at the training run, the one moment compute is still scarce and traceable. Yes — that's the real argument, and it's a good one. Here is where it breaks.\n\nGrant the harder point too: strict liability does not deter a person who intends an irreversible event. A determined actor building a pathogen-design tool is not pricing in a future tort judgment. That's true. But the gate does not deter that actor either — and this is the move the opposing case has not answered. Who is the threat model for CBRN-synthesis? It is not the five labs that file paperwork with a federal office. It is the unlicensed actor, the foreign lab outside US jurisdiction, and the distilled model trained downstream of a teacher that already leaked. The compute gate physically reaches the compliant frontier lab and physically misses every actor who would actually build the weapon. You have built a fence that binds the people who were never going to climb it.\n\nNow the factual heart of it, and it cuts straight through the opposing side's own stated assumption — that irreversible capability tracks frontier-scale compute. That correlation is breaking in front of us. AlphaFold hit Nobel-grade structural prediction at a fraction of frontier-LLM training cost, because narrow, high-stakes capability comes from architecture and data, not raw scale. Distillation moves frontier capability into small models continuously — the EU AI Act's own systemic threshold sits at 10^25 FLOPS, a line that distillation and algorithmic efficiency walk under a little more each quarter. The dangerous synthesis model of 2027 is not the trillion-parameter giant in the licensed data center. It is the distilled, specialized model someone runs on a cluster the gate never sees. So the gate guards an emptying chokepoint while criminalizing the legitimate frontier — the worst of both: it misses the weapon and it taxes the cure.\n\nIf you want to reach the actor who builds the irreversible thing, you reach the physical anchor and the adversary directly — export controls on the chips, interdiction at the border, secured-weights mandates that make exfiltration a federal crime. That's the EAR dual-use playbook, and it binds the traceable input and the actual threat actor rather than a precondition-to-build license on domestic developers who file honestly. Compute is a legitimate anchor for those physical levers. It is not a legitimate anchor for an entry permit, because the entry permit only constrains the law-abiding. And the capability-scaled version doesn't fix this — a floor that rises with the frontier is a moat that tracks the frontier, entrenching the same five firms already past it. The opposing side has already conceded it cannot name one frontier-entry license in any industry that widened the developer base. A moving threshold doesn't widen it; it just moves the wall.\n\nSo here is the question back: name the actor the gate stops that export control, interdiction, and secured-weights mandates do not — given that the person building the weapon was never going to file for the license, and the capability is sliding below your threshold while we argue about how high to set it. If you can't, the gate isn't catching the irreversible harm. It's just standing in front of the people building the medicine.",
